## 1. G0 — vendor pricing capture (keyless GETs; page had moved)
============================================================
The packet names the vendor pricing page. The former path <https://jina.ai/pricing/>
returned HTTP 404 (the page moved), so the vendor's own sitemap was read (HTTP 200)
listing <https://jina.ai/api-dashboard/pricing/>, which returned HTTP 200 and is the
page quoted below. The page is a Vue SPA: its rate table is server-rendered (quoted
from the HTML), while the top-up pack prices are loaded by the page itself from
<https://dash.jina.ai/api/v1/product> (keyless, HTTP 200) — quoted below. This is one
egress shape to one vendor; the extra GETs are the "page moved" discovery plus the
page's own data source, reported as a deviation in the report.

### 1a. GET /pricing/ -> moved
$ curl -sSL --max-time 30 -o jina_pricing_20260925.html -w 'HTTP=%{http_code} url=%{url_effective} bytes=%{size_download}' https://jina.ai/pricing/
HTTP=404 url=https://jina.ai/pricing/ bytes=27047

### 1b. GET /api-dashboard/pricing/ -> the current pricing page
$ curl -sSL --max-time 30 -o jina_pricing_dash_20260925.html -w 'HTTP=%{http_code} url=%{url_effective} bytes=%{size_download}' https://jina.ai/api-dashboard/pricing/
HTTP=200 url=https://jina.ai/api-dashboard/pricing/ bytes=83428
$ grep -oiE '<title>[^<]*</title>' jina_pricing_dash_20260925.html
<title>API Pricing - Jina AI</title>

### 1c. Quoted Search API figure + billing unit (verbatim from the page's rate table)
The vendor's table labels the s.jina.ai row "Reader API" with description "Search the
web and convert results to LLM-friendly text" and the token-usage column quoted:
  Every request costs a fixed number of tokens, starting from 10,000 tokens
API endpoint cell: https://s.jina.ai
=> the Search API bills PER REQUEST in the vendor's token credit unit (min 10,000 tokens).

### 1d. Quoted credit -> USD conversion (page's top-up data source, keyless)
$ curl -sSL --max-time 30 -H 'Accept: application/json' https://dash.jina.ai/api/v1/product
HTTP=200 bytes=5242
The page's own bundle fetches this and filters to the two currently-offered packs
(yx=[price_1RLbwHEoTOtb5eZvZP7kjljR, price_1RLbxCEoTOtb5eZvngWeE0jf]); verbatim records:
  {"price_id":"price_1RLbwHEoTOtb5eZvZP7kjljR","product_name":"1B tokens","price":50.0,"currency":"USD,EUR","token_quantity":1000000000,...}
  {"price_id":"price_1RLbxCEoTOtb5eZvngWeE0jf","product_name":"11B tokens","price":500.0,"currency":"USD,EUR","token_quantity":11000000000,...}
=> base pack 1B tokens = $50.00 USD -> $0.05 per 1M tokens
   (the other offered pack is $0.0455/1M; $0.05 is the conservative upper bound).
   All 17 packs returned span $0.0169-$1.00 per 1M (pack-price spread is a report finding).

### 1e. In-tree no-cost premise re-verify (BASE b041ee9, enrich/)
The packet's premise "jina.py carries NO cost/USD/usage symbol" is TRUE, but the wider
premise "enrich/ has no cost symbol" is FALSE: synthesize.py already carries the
OpenCodeGo text-call rates + estimate_text_cost. Those are a DIFFERENT vendor/unit, so
the Jina rate is added beside the Jina client (extension of the same package's cost
family), never a duplicate of estimate_text_cost. Verbatim BASE grep:


